      "The city archive had drafted three apologies before anyone asked whether the residents wanted one. In the first, the institution regretted that records had been misplaced; in the second, it acknowledged that its own filing policy had made those records inaccessible; in the third, it said that people had been denied the chance to contest decisions made in their names. The differences looked microscopic on the page. In the public hearing, they were anything but. An apology that merely registered regret could be uttered without identifying an actor, whereas the third version came perilously close to admitting a wrong.",
