      "In the hearing's second session, a former clerk described a mundane obstacle: staff had been trained to recognise missing folders as a logistical difficulty, never as a loss of someone's right to appeal. Her account complicated the tempting search for one villain. The faulty policy had been repeated by employees who did not know its origin and defended by supervisors who feared reopening thousands of decisions. Residents, in turn, were unwilling to let the diffusion of responsibility become an excuse for its disappearance. The hearing thus moved from asking whether anyone had intended harm to asking how a system could repeatedly produce it without correction.",
