      "The committee’s original letter began, ‘Your account does not accord with the records available to us.’ The revision said, ‘We have been unable to reconcile your account with the records currently available.’ A sceptic might dismiss the alteration as the sort of diplomatic varnish that institutions apply to avoid saying anything plainly. Yet the second sentence does something precise: it locates the failure to reconcile in the committee’s present knowledge, not necessarily in the recipient’s truthfulness. The distinction becomes vital when the records themselves are incomplete.",
