      "The editing group also debated whether the word ‘unable’ weakened the committee's authority. In one sense it did: it acknowledged that the body had not resolved a discrepancy. In another, it made the conclusion more robust by specifying the remaining work. A sentence that says ‘you are wrong’ offers no route for new evidence to matter, whereas a sentence about reconciliation can be revisited when records surface. That revisability was not a rhetorical concession but an epistemic commitment. It required the committee to publish the standard by which it was prepared to change its position.",
      "When the letters were read aloud, the courteous revision sounded firmer than the original. Its commitments could be tested: the committee would disclose the evidence relied upon, answer specific objections and publish a chronology of decisions. The first letter, by contrast, asked the other party to do all the work. Not every softening of a sentence conceals a retreat; sometimes precision is the very thing that makes a request harder to evade."
