    "paragraphs": [
      "The opening of Lena Vale’s novel appears to offer a reliable narrator: an elderly cartographer describing a town in which every street has a name but no one agrees on the boundaries. Only later does the reader notice that the narrator’s certainty belongs to his younger self, whose thoughts seep into the narration without quotation marks. ‘The council had acted fairly; surely they had’, he recalls while recounting a decision he now regrets. The sentence has the grammar of retrospect and the temperature of a conviction not yet tested. That double perspective makes the book more disquieting than a straightforward confession.",
      "Vale neither labels each shift in viewpoint nor leaves the reader helpless. A repeated image of a brass compass marks moments when the narrator substitutes inherited stories for direct observation. In one chapter a neighbour’s silence seems proof of hostility; two chapters later the same silence acquires the texture of grief. The novelist does not announce the correction. She lets the older voice falter just enough for the earlier interpretation to become visible as an interpretation. Readers who demand a single, transparent consciousness may call this evasive. Yet its uncertainty is carefully engineered, not accidental.",
      "Vale has said in interviews that she did not set out to write a puzzle. That claim need not settle the question of how readers experience the work. A novel can generate effects its author did not consciously plan, and critical interpretation should not be reduced to reconstructing intention. Still, the recurrence of the compass and the controlled shifts in free indirect discourse provide more than a critic's imaginative projection. They are material features of the text that explain why one interpretation carries more weight than another. Ambiguity, in this sense, is not the absence of structure but its carefully distributed pressure.",
      "The final pages offer no grand reconciliation. The cartographer redraws a disputed path but leaves the old map hanging in his office. It is a modest act of revision rather than a triumph over error. The novel’s achievement lies in making us experience the speed with which a story becomes a fact when it is told in a voice we trust."
    ]
