      "In the final scene of The Unfinished Guest, a woman is offered an explanation for her brother’s disappearance. She does not respond. Instead, she folds the letter, lays it beside an empty cup and walks out through the audience. The production lasts ninety minutes; this exit takes less than ten seconds. So sudden is the movement that the silence which follows feels imposed on the spectators rather than chosen by the character. The director has resisted the temptation to turn an unresolved absence into a theatrical revelation.",
      "At the preview, one reviewer complained that the gesture withheld catharsis. Another called it an act of emancipation: a character declines to accept an answer designed principally to settle someone else’s curiosity. Both interpretations are defensible, but neither explains the sound design. Moments before the exit, the room’s familiar hum disappears, leaving every shift in the audience audible. It is the spectators who supply the noise of the scene. Their discomfort becomes part of the action, and the supposed ending continues after the performer has gone.",
      "Several audience members later described feeling irritated rather than moved. The director welcomed the response but resisted claiming it proved the production's success. Irritation could register resistance to an ethical demand, or it could expose a clumsy formal choice. A review that simply celebrates discomfort as evidence of depth evades that distinction. The more persuasive accounts examined exactly when the sound faded, how the performer crossed the seating area and what the audience was able to see. Aesthetic judgement became sharper when it treated the emotional reaction as something to explain, not as an automatic endorsement.",
      "The programme notes refuse to decide whether the letter is true. This is not laziness. The production asks what a revelation is worth when the person to whom it is offered has not requested it. By leaving the explanation unread, the character deprives the audience of the authority it assumed it possessed. The night I attended, applause began unevenly, as though people were unsure whether they had been released from the performance."
