    "level": "C2",
    "unit": 6,
    "moduleId": "c2en-reading-24",
    "title": "Correcting a Friend Without Winning",
    "source": "Texte original — C2 — personal letter",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Dear Noor, I have rewritten this letter three times, mostly because every first sentence sounded like a verdict. When you said at dinner that I had dismissed your project, I immediately wanted to explain what I meant. The explanation was not invented; I was thinking about the deadlines, not the idea itself. But I can see now that my remark was made in front of people whose opinion matters to you, and that the setting changed what the remark did. Intention is part of the story, though it is not the whole of its effect.",
      "I wish I had asked what sort of feedback you wanted before offering any. I also wish you had told me at the table that the comment had hurt, though I recognise that asking you to respond publicly would have placed another burden on you. This is where my earlier drafts kept going wrong: each apology smuggled in a demand that you acknowledge my good intentions. I am trying to leave that demand out. You do not owe me reassurance in return for an admission that I spoke carelessly.",
      "A mutual friend suggested that you would be reassured if I explained my good intentions in person. I hesitated. A meeting could provide repair, but it could also become another stage on which you were expected to perform acceptance. I decided that an invitation with an explicit possibility of refusal would be more respectful than a surprise visit. This small decision aligns the letter's form with its claim: I can acknowledge an effect I caused without controlling your response. Apology, here, means making room for your account to remain different.",
      "If you want to talk, I would like to hear which part of the evening felt most difficult. If you would rather not, I will respect that too. I am not asking you to agree with my account. I am asking whether there is a way to make future conversations less costly for both of us."
    ]
